When Eruan was a child,  there were many stories told .. of Feanor and
the Silmarils, of Turgon and the hidden city of Gondolin, and the
incomparable tale of Beren and Luthien and the Great Jewel.

But no story did he remember so well, and no story was so emphasized,
as the story of Ellora the Elven ranger, who had ventured into the
mines in the service of their deity.  Of the battles she had fought and
her final mysterious disappearance. What happened to her or whether she
lived or died, none could say.  Only her diary had ever been found by a
subsequent expedition.  All the time he grew up, and all the time he
was training to be a ranger, he was reminded of Ellora's gallant
attempt and encouraged to do likewise, should he be selected to follow
in her footsteps in the Dungeons of Doom.

Every year a new class of elven warriors, priests, wizards and rangers
completed their training passed to manhood/womanhood. Every year, as
demanded by the god, one of their number would be selected by lot to
enter the Dungeon of Doom to retrieve the amulet for his greater glory.
 The one selected attended his funeral while still alive, for even if
successful the one would never be seen among his mortal kin again.  And
so it was that with trepidation, but also with excitement, that Eruan
wound up his mortal affairs, said goodbye to his mother and brothers,
gathered his bow and arrows, and set off into the dungeons of doom, the
latest elven ranger to make the attempt ...

... and this is why, 120000 turns later, when he at last banged the
Amulet on the Altar,  he said, "This one's for Ellora".

--------------------
In the interest of brevity, I'm going to skip the long story and just
hit the high points.

1.  Game-breaking moment:  A werejackal death-drops a plate mail on
Dlvl 4, giving me an early armor class of -5.
This was game-breaking because not only did it give me an excellent
starting AC but it also forced a change in tactics that proved to be
game-winners.  Previously, I had used a bow and arrows for ranged
combat, but the heavy weight of plate made this infeasible.  After much
consideration,  I chucked the bow and arrows into  a box and made do
with 12 daggers as my ranged weapons.

This is another reason why this game is dedicated to Ellora -- because
I emulated Krysia's tactic of throwing daggers for ranged attacks, and
this proved to be an excellent choice. Not only did they cause
considerable damage, not only could I throw up to 4 a turn,  but the
daggers never seemed to disappear as arrows or crossbow bolts did.  I
kept the same 17 daggers throughout the game and only lost one -- and I
think that I lost it.

The daggers made a lot of enemies who were previously very problematic
almost easy.  Pack of soldier ants?   Daggers away!  Problem solved.
Nymph? Daggers away! Problem solved.  Succubus?  ... you get the idea.


I'm beginning to realize that if Nethack gives you something in
abundance it's because it's a very useful tool that will keep you alive
when nothing else will. Daggers, wands of digging, wands of teleport,
scrolls of teleport -- all are exceptionally useful and all appear in
quantity.

In any case,  the daggers -- later enchanted to +5 to make the Deadly
Daggers of Doom -- proved to be my primary missile weapon for most of
the game, and they stood me in good stead right up to the end levels.
Thanks, Kris!

1A) Second game-breaker: Altar on level 1, allowing me to find uncursed
armor in a hurry when my dog got paralyzed.

2. Moment I knew the RNG had given up:  Finding a bones pile on level
22 with Greyswandir AND Mjollnir AND gray dragon scale armor AND an
amulet of reflection.  This made me want to get down and weep for joy.


Of course,  I promptly picked up grayswandir and, you guessed it, it
was cursed.  I then read a scroll which turned out to be punishment.
So here I am on a desecrated altar with a cursed weapon in hand and a
ball and chain attached. It was at this point I decided I had played
enough for the night and went to bed.  Next day, a scroll of remove
curse solved both problems and the game goes on.

3.  Stupid blunder #1:  Throwing my Longbow of Artemis (game is 3.4.X
of some sort) into the drink.  Recovered by polymorphing into a Xorn
and pulling it back.

4. Stupid blunder #2: Eating a tengu at the bottom of the dungeon. I
already had rings of teleport and TC. I had no need for the inherent
intrinsic.  I KNEW I didn't need it, but I ate it anyway.  Result ?
Uncontrolled teleportitis.  I had to keep my ring o' TC on, sacrificing
a ring slot, for most of the rest of the game.

4A. Stupid blunder #3: Displace 2nd pet into a lethal trap. Took a lot
of sacrificing to mollify game deity.  In that time, no holy water, no
protection from tainted food, no protection from hunger.

5. New tactic:  Use wand of enlightenment to identify rings. If it was
uncursed and nothing obvious happened when I put it on, zap wand of
enlightenment to view my intrinsics and see what changed.

6. The RNG pays me back for it's earlier generosity department: Never
saw a single scroll of taming during the entire game. Not once.  Nor a
scroll of charging unless I wished for it or wrote it myself.  I did,
however, encounter 15 air elementals (!!) in Gehennom and the Dungeons
and 8 mind flayers.  So I guess it all balances out in the end.

7. Not engulfed once on plane of air, as ring of conflict and wand of
teleportation stand me in good stead.

8.  You're-never-invulnerable department:  Encountered an Archon on
plane of fire and foolishly engaged it in melee combat.
Oh no! He's using the touch of death!
GREAT SH****!
Lucky for you, it didn't work!
*Begins to breathe again*

Just goes to show again -- no matter how many hit points you have, no
matter how low your AC, no matter your experience level, negligence can
kill. I think it wouldn't have worked in any case do to magic
resistance, but will have to check to be sure.

8. New tactic: Carried shield of reflection in BoH in case I needed to
wear an amulet of LS or the amulet of Yendor. This served me in good
stead on the plane of water when I absolutely COULD NOT find the
portal, despite using 2 scrolls and 1 I wrote myself.  I eventually
slipped on the amulet and used it's heat to clue in on where the portal
was.  When it was "hot" I read one last written scroll and found that
the portal was hidden under a gem stone, and escaped to astral.

8. Is it just me, or does anyone else  think the end game resembles
American football?  Think of the Amulet as the football, yourself as a
running back, the monsters as "defensive tackles", and the portal/altar
as the end zone.    The objective is not so much to fight one's way
through as to "find the daylight" and rush through a hole in the
defense, avoiding all unnecessary combat.

9. On astral,  go left. Encounter death. He gets in one touch, but I
kill him with a +3 glaive.  Use neither conflict nor cockatrice
throughout level.  After fighting my way through 8 angels and 13 or so
priests, run into altar room and it is the correct one.

